## Tool Class — `WeatherTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the current weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name, e.g. 'Seattle'")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching forecast for {City}", city);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/data/2.5/weather?q={city}&appid=demo&units=metric",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the UV index for given coordinates.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUvIndex(
        [Description("Latitude")] double lat,
        [Description("Longitude")] double lon,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/data/2.5/uvi?lat={lat}&lon={lon}&appid=demo",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

## `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC channel
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient<WeatherTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.openweathermap.org");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | Class type | How services arrive |
|-------|-----------|---------------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static class (shown above) | Services injected via primary constructor — class is resolved from DI per invocation |
| **Method parameter injection** | Static class | DI services appear as method parameters alongside tool parameters |

**Key rule:** The SDK automatically distinguishes tool parameters from injected services. Types like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, and `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>` are **injected by the framework** and never appear in the tool's JSON schema shown to the LLM. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed.

For the constructor style above, `AddHttpClient<WeatherTools>()` registers both the `HttpClient` and the tool class itself in DI. The SDK resolves `WeatherTools` from the container, so all constructor dependencies are satisfied automatically.

To scaffold this from scratch:

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n WeatherMcpServer
cd WeatherMcpServer
dotnet build && dotnet run
```
